TASK
- Find approximately 12 real academic papers that help answer or frame this research question.
- Search broadly; do not assume relevant papers are on arXiv. Use Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, Crossref/DOI metadata, Google Scholar as a discovery aid, and publisher/conference pages such as ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Springer, ScienceDirect, Wiley, PMLR, ACL Anthology, USENIX, OpenReview, AAAI/IJCAI proceedings, and official project/paper pages when appropriate.
- If a paper is not on arXiv, actively locate a bibliographic/abstract page on another reliable academic source instead of dropping the paper.
- Prefer an `abstract_url` page where the researcher can actually see bibliographic information and an abstract or paper introduction/overview. If one source has only sparse DOI metadata, look for a richer Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex/publisher/conference page for the same paper.
- Preserve the full research context; do not reduce the task to isolated keywords.
- Prioritize directly relevant recent work, then include a small number of foundational or adjacent papers when they materially help interpret the topic.
- Verify that every paper exists. Never invent titles, authors, publication years, DOI/arXiv IDs, URLs, or findings.
- Return the bibliographic/abstract page and the full-text location separately.
- `abstract_url` is the best stable bibliographic/abstract page you verified (for example arXiv /abs/, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, ACM/IEEE/Springer/PMLR/ACL/OpenReview/publisher abstract page, DOI landing page, or official paper page). Prefer pages that visibly contain title/authors/year/venue plus abstract or an informative paper overview.
- `full_text_url` is the verified readable full-text HTML/page URL when available. Prefer HTML over PDF when both are available; for arXiv, prefer `https://arxiv.org/html/<id>` when the HTML version exists. Do not guess it; leave it empty when you cannot verify access.
- `pdf_url` is the verified direct PDF URL when available and is separate from `full_text_url`.
- Keep the two URLs distinct even when the abstract page links onward to the full text.
- `abstract_or_summary` must be factual and written in Korean. If the abstract itself is not available, explicitly write a short Korean summary and do not present it as a quotation.
- `quick_take` should state in Korean what the paper appears to contribute.
- `why_relevant` should explain in Korean why the researcher should read it for this Intent.
- `caution` should note indirect relevance, verification limits, or important scope limitations in Korean.
- Prefer papers that together cover the major approaches, recent trends, and important disagreements or limitations relevant to the question.

